## [ISSUE] One failing cancel request stops every other cancellation, on every run

**Severity:** HIGH - one bad reminder stops all deferred cancellations, so emails for cancelled bookings go out again (the bug this PR is meant to fix).

### The Problem

```ts
try {
  const workflowRemindersToDelete = [];
  for (const reminder of remindersToCancel) {
    await client.request({ url: "/v3/user/scheduled_sends", method: "POST",
      body: { batch_id: reminder.referenceId, status: "cancel" } });
    const workflowReminderToDelete = prisma.workflowReminder.delete({ where: { id: reminder.id } });
    workflowRemindersToDelete.push(workflowReminderToDelete);
  }
  await Promise.all(workflowRemindersToDelete);
} catch (error) {
  console.log(`Error cancelling scheduled Emails: ${error}`);
}
```

The whole loop sits inside one `try`. If any `client.request` throws, control jumps to the `catch`. Two things follow:

1. Every reminder after the failing one is skipped.
2. `Promise.all(workflowRemindersToDelete)` never runs. Prisma query builders are lazy: `prisma.workflowReminder.delete(...)` only runs the query when it is awaited or `.then` is called ([Prisma docs: "Prisma Client queries are lazy / PrismaPromise"](https://www.prisma.io/docs/concepts/components/prisma-client/crud)). So reminders that SendGrid did cancel are **not** deleted either.

### Theory / Proof

`findMany` has no `orderBy`, but in practice Postgres returns rows in a stable order. On the next run (15 minutes later), the reminders that were cancelled but not deleted come back first. SendGrid rejects a second `POST /v3/user/scheduled_sends` for a `batch_id` that already has a status with HTTP 400 (the docs list `"batch id already has a status"` as an error). That new failure aborts the loop again, before it reaches the reminders that were never cancelled. The loop never gets past this point. Each cancelled email whose send time falls in the window goes out.

Ways to trigger the first failure are easy to reach:
- a transient SendGrid 5xx or 429,
- the 100-pending-cancellation cap described in the PR,
- a double cancel (see the separate comment about `handleNewBooking` cancelling at SendGrid immediately without clearing the `cancelled` flag),
- a stale or invalid `batch_id`.

### What happens today vs. what will break

With zero or one flagged reminder per run and no SendGrid errors, this works. After the first error, every later run hits the same poisoned entry until its `scheduledDate` passes and line 34 deletes it. Every cancellation in that window is lost.

### Research

The existing scheduling loop in this same file (lines 105-222) already does this correctly: it puts `try/catch` **inside** the `for` so one bad reminder cannot block the others. The cancellation loop should do the same.

### Recommendation

Handle each reminder on its own, and delete the row right away when the cancel succeeds or when SendGrid says the batch already has a status:

```ts
for (const reminder of remindersToCancel) {
  try {
    if (reminder.referenceId) {
      await client.request({
        url: "/v3/user/scheduled_sends",
        method: "POST",
        body: { batch_id: reminder.referenceId, status: "cancel" },
      });
    }
    await prisma.workflowReminder.delete({ where: { id: reminder.id } });
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`Error cancelling scheduled Email ${reminder.id}: ${error}`);
  }
}
```

## [ISSUE] `immediateDelete` cancels at SendGrid but leaves the DB row, which leads to a second cancel

**Severity:** MEDIUM - leads to duplicate cancel requests that fail at SendGrid and stop the cron cancellation loop.

### The Problem

```ts
if (immediateDelete) {
  await client.request({ url: "/v3/user/scheduled_sends", method: "POST",
    body: { batch_id: referenceId, status: "cancel" } });
  return;
}
```

After a successful cancel, the `WorkflowReminder` row is neither deleted nor updated.

### Theory / Proof

Here is a reachable sequence:
1. The host clicks "Request reschedule". `bookings.tsx:490` calls `deleteScheduledEmailReminder(id, refId)`, which sets `cancelled: true`. The booking becomes `CANCELLED`.
2. The attendee opens the reschedule link and books a new slot. `getOriginalRescheduledBooking` (`handleNewBooking.ts:745`) accepts `CANCELLED` bookings and now loads `workflowReminders: true`, including the already-flagged rows.
3. `handleNewBooking.ts:968` calls this function with `immediateDelete = true`. SendGrid cancels the batch, and the row still has `cancelled: true`.
4. When the send time is less than 1 hour away, the cron (`scheduleEmailReminders.ts:44`) picks the row up and posts a second cancel for the same `batch_id`. SendGrid rejects it (the batch already has a status). Because the cron wraps the whole loop in a single `try`, this also stops the cancellation of every other flagged reminder in that run.

Even without step 1, rows cancelled this way stay in the table with `scheduled: true` and are only removed by the cleanup at `scheduleEmailReminders.ts:34` after their send time.

### Recommendation

Delete the row once the immediate cancel succeeds. For workflow and step deletion the cascade would remove it anyway, so this is harmless there:

```ts
if (immediateDelete) {
  await client.request({
    url: "/v3/user/scheduled_sends",
    method: "POST",
    body: { batch_id: referenceId, status: "cancel" },
  });
  await prisma.workflowReminder.deleteMany({ where: { id: reminderId } });
  return;
}
```

Also skip reminders that are already flagged (`if (reminder.cancelled) return;`) when handling a reschedule.

## [ISSUE] Reminder cancellation is now fire-and-forget in the cancel handler

**Severity:** MEDIUM - on serverless deployments the `cancelled: true` update may never be written, so the cron never cancels the email.

### The Problem

```ts
updatedBookings.forEach((booking) => {
  booking.workflowReminders.forEach((reminder) => {
    if (reminder.method === WorkflowMethods.EMAIL) {
      deleteScheduledEmailReminder(reminder.id, reminder.referenceId);   // not awaited
    } else if (reminder.method === WorkflowMethods.SMS) {
      deleteScheduledSMSReminder(reminder.id, reminder.referenceId);     // not awaited
    }
  });
});

const prismaPromises: Promise<unknown>[] = [attendeeDeletes, bookingReferenceDeletes];
```

Before this PR, the `prisma.workflowReminder.deleteMany` calls were collected into `remindersToDelete` and awaited through `Promise.all(prismaPromises...)`. Now the only thing that records the cancellation (`prisma.workflowReminder.update({ data: { cancelled: true } })`) runs inside a promise nobody awaits.

### Theory / Proof

With the new design, the database flag is the **only** record that the email must be cancelled. Nothing is sent to SendGrid at cancel time. If that write is lost, the email is sent.

On Vercel, the function can be frozen or shut down once the response is sent. Vercel's docs say work still running after the response is not guaranteed to finish unless you use `waitUntil`. Here the handler awaits `apiDeletes` and `sendCancelledEmails(evt)` first, which usually gives the unawaited updates time to finish. That is timing luck, not a guarantee. Errors from these calls are also invisible to the caller.

The same pattern appears in `bookings.tsx:486-494` (`requestReschedule`), where `await Promise.all(remindersToDelete)` was removed, and in `handleNewBooking.ts:964-975`. There the `try/catch` around a `forEach` of async calls can never catch anything.

### Recommendation

Collect and await the promises:

```ts
const reminderCancellations = updatedBookings.flatMap((booking) =>
  booking.workflowReminders.map((reminder) => {
    if (reminder.method === WorkflowMethods.EMAIL) {
      return deleteScheduledEmailReminder(reminder.id, reminder.referenceId);
    } else if (reminder.method === WorkflowMethods.SMS) {
      return deleteScheduledSMSReminder(reminder.id, reminder.referenceId);
    }
  })
);

await Promise.all([attendeeDeletes, bookingReferenceDeletes, ...reminderCancellations, ...apiDeletes]);
```
